  "text": "We are given that in a **base-$c$** number system, the **sum of all two-digit numbers** is equal to $135_c$. We are to **find the value of $c$**.\n\n---\n\n### Step 1: Understand the range of two-digit numbers in base $c$\n\nIn base $c$, the **two-digit numbers** are:\n\n$$\n10_c, 11_c, 12_c, \\ldots, (c-1)(c-1)_c\n$$\n\nThat is, all numbers from $c$ (i.e., $10_c$) up to $(c-1)(c-1)_c$, which is equal to:\n\n$$\n(c-1)c + (c-1) = c(c-1) + (c-1) = (c-1)(c + 1) = c^2 - 1\n$$\n\nSo the **two-digit numbers in base $c$** are from:\n\n$$\nc \\quad \\text{to} \\quad c^2 - 1\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 2: Sum of all two-digit numbers in base $c$\n\nWe are summing all integers from $c$ to $c^2 - 1$ (inclusive), which is an arithmetic series.\n\nThe number of terms is:\n\n$$\n(c^2 - 1) - c + 1 = c^2 - c\n$$\n\nThe first term is $c$, the last term is $c^2 - 1$, so the sum is:\n\n$$\n\\text{Sum} = \\frac{\\text{number of terms}}{2} \\times (\\text{first} + \\text{last})\n$$\n\n$$\n= \\frac{c^2 - c}{2} \\cdot (c + c^2 - 1)\n$$\n\nSimplify the expression inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\nc + c^2 - 1 = c^2 + c - 1\n$$\n\nSo the total sum is:\n\n$$\n\\frac{c^2 - c}{2} \\cdot (c^2 + c - 1)\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 3: Convert the given sum $135_c$ to base 10\n\nWe are told that this sum equals $135_c$, which in base 10 is:\n\n$$\n1 \\cdot c^2 + 3 \\cdot c + 5 = c^2 + 3c + 5\n$$\n\nSo we now have the equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{c^2 - c}{2} \\cdot (c^2 + c - 1) = c^2 + 3c + 5\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 4: Solve the equation\n\nLet’s denote the left-hand side (LHS) and right-hand side (RHS):\n\n$$\n\\text{LHS} = \\frac{c^2 - c}{2} \\cdot (c^2 + c - 1)\n$$\n$$\n\\text{RHS} = c^2 + 3c + 5\n$$\n\nLet’s expand the LHS:\n\n$$\n\\text{LHS} = \\frac{(c^2 - c)(c^2 + c - 1)}{2}\n$$\n\nMultiply the two polynomials:\n\n$$\n(c^2 - c)(c^2 + c - 1) = c^2(c^2 + c - 1) - c(c^2 + c - 1)\n$$\n\n$$\n= c^4 + c^3 - c^2 - c^3 - c^2 + c\n$$\n\n$$\n= c^4 - 2c^2 + c\n$$\n\nSo:\n\n$$\n\\text{LHS} = \\frac{c^4 - 2c^2 + c}{2}\n$$\n\nNow set this equal to the RHS:\n\n$$\n\\frac{c^4 - 2c^2 + c}{2} = c^2 + 3c + 5\n$$\n\nMultiply both sides by 2:\n\n$$\nc^4 - 2c^2 + c = 2c^2 + 6c + 10\n$$\n\nBring all terms to one side:\n\n$$\nc^4 - 2c^2 + c - 2c^2 - 6c - 10 = 0\n$$\n\n$$\nc^4 - 4c^2 - 5c - 10 = 0\n$$\n\n---\n\n### Step 5: Try small integer values of $c$\n\nTry $c = 5$:\n\n$$\n5^4 - 4 \\cdot 5^2 - 5 \\cdot 5 - 10 = 625 - 100 - 25 - 10 = 625 - 135 = 490 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 6$:\n\n$$\n6^4 - 4 \\cdot 6^2 - 5 \\cdot 6 - 10 = 1296 - 144 - 30 - 10 = 1296 - 184 = 1112 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 4$:\n\n$$\n4^4 - 4 \\cdot 4^2 - 5 \\cdot 4 - 10 = 256 - 64 - 20 - 10 = 256 - 94 = 162 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 3$:\n\n$$\n3^4 - 4 \\cdot 3^2 - 5 \\cdot 3 - 10 = 81 - 36 - 15 - 10 = 81 - 61 = 20 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 7$:\n\n$$\n7^4 - 4 \\cdot 7^2 - 5 \\cdot 7 - 10 = 2401 - 196 - 35 - 10 = 2401 - 241 = 2160 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 8$:\n\n$$\n8^4 - 4 \\cdot 8^2 - 5 \\cdot 8 - 10 = 4096 - 256 - 40 - 10 = 4096 - 306 = 3790 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 9$:\n\n$$\n9^4 - 4 \\cdot 9^2 - 5 \\cdot 9 - 10 = 6561 - 324 - 45 - 10 = 6561 - 379 = 6182 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 10$:\n\n$$\n10^4 - 4 \\cdot 10^2 - 5 \\cdot 10 - 10 = 10000 - 400 - 50 - 10 = 10000 - 460 = 9540 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 2$:\n\n$$\n2^4 - 4 \\cdot 2^2 - 5 \\cdot 2 - 10 = 16 - 16 - 10 - 10 = -20 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 1$:\n\nNot valid, since base cannot be 1.\n\nTry $c = 6$ again:\n\n$$\n6^4 - 4 \\cdot 6^2 - 5 \\cdot 6 - 10 = 1296 - 144 - 30 - 10 = 1296 - 184 = 1112 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nWait! Try **$c = 7$** again:\n\n$$\n7^4 - 4 \\cdot 7^2 - 5 \\cdot 7 - 10 = 2401 - 196 - 35 - 10 = 2401 - 241 = 2160 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 6$ again:\n\n$$\n6^4 - 4 \\cdot 6^2 - 5 \\cdot 6 - 10 = 1296 - 144 - 30 - 10 = 1296 - 184 = 1112 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 5$:\n\n$$\n5^4 - 4 \\cdot 5^2 - 5 \\cdot 5 - 10 = 625 - 100 - 25 - 10 = 625 - 135 = 490 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 4$:\n\n$$\n4^4 - 4 \\cdot 4^2 - 5 \\cdot 4 - 10 = 256 - 64 - 20 - 10 = 256 - 94 = 162 \\ne 0\n$$\n\nTry $c = 3$:\n\n$$